Private schools in Scotland maintain a track record of academic superiority, and retention leads to outstanding exam results in 2018, which is a further increase in personal and comprehensive success in sports, the arts, music and other areas of hard work.
As represented by the Scottish Council of Private Schools (SCIS), many of these schools with over 30,000 students are committed to providing the best service to students and their parents.
The private school aims to prepare its students for adult and higher education, as well-determined as a chosen career and position as a global citizen.
As an educational institution that is able to plan and execute a bespoke school curriculum, we continue to witness that modern language continues to be a popular and desired elective among schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak in the language he appreciates, it's going to get into his head.
If you speak in his own language, it's going to be etched in his heart."
This strongly tells us that when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other different countries, we can’t rely solely on English.
The results of this year's recent exams show that the language ranks first in the league's table with the highest pass rates within an independent school.
Sixty-six percent of students who studied a foreign language all received a high-grade A credit.
According to data collected from 74 member schools in SCIS, 72 percent of students received A credits in Mandarin, 72 percent of German-speaking students, 69 percent of French-speaking students, and 63 percent of Spanish-educated students.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland are supporting foreign languages as an essential skill for children and young people in the future.
The current language is a choice of subjects and is being treated in terms such as STEM (science, technology, engineering, and mathematics) in an independent school curriculum.
According to a survey conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014, 17 per cent are attributable to a lack of language skills because employers have struggled to suffocate.
As a result, language skills are becoming increasingly necessary for young people to prepare them for their future careers.
With more future job opportunities that require a language, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
No matter which profession you choose, a person who learns a second language will have the practical advantage of this lifelong ability in the future.
Being able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically put the multi-lingual people ahead of the competition.
According to YouGov’s poll of more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 per cent don’t speak a good enough foreign language to be able to talk, making it the only language in which French is used at a double-digit rate of 15 per cent.
That’s why it’s so important to invest in language education in children today.
The use of multi-lingual languages, especially in developing countries, will give children a better opportunity to find meaningful jobs.
There will be a variety of languages taught by schools within Scotland.
Many schools will focus on a more classical modern language, while others will teach the languages considered to be the most important in the UK, such as Mandarin or Japanese, when looking forward to the 2020s.
No matter what kind of interest your child is, there will always be many languages to choose from within an independent school, along with our faculty who are experts in this field.
Scotland’s independent schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children for anything in the future and provide them with the skills needed for their success.
In the global business environment, it is undeniable that language continues to be very important for a country’s future, so this must be reflected in education.
In practice, modern languages should really be considered "the ability to communicate internationally."
The independent schools will continue to provide these choices, diversity and excellence to young people in Scotland.
It's the faire.
John Edward is director of the Scottish Independent School Board.
