"Impossivability" to unilaterally disarmament without trust in North Korea
North Korea’s foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that continued sanctions are deepening distrust of the U.S. and there’s absolutely no way for North Korea to unilaterally give up its nuclear weapons under those circumstances.
Ri Yong-ho said at the World Organization's annual general meeting that North Korea has taken "significant friendly measures" over the past year, including suspending nuclear and missile tests, dismantling nuclear test sites, and vowing not to escalate nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But I don’t see a corresponding response from the United States,” he said.
"If there is no trust in the United States, there will be no confidence in our national security, and there will be no unilateral disarmament in such a situation."
Foreign Minister Lee reaffirmed North Korea’s familiar dissatisfaction with U.S. resistance to “step-by-step” escalation to be compensatory as North Korea takes incremental steps, but his comments seemed meaningful in that he did not reject unilateral escalation as North Korea did in the past.
At the first summit between U.S. and North Korean leaders in Singapore on the 12th of last month, South Korean Foreign Minister Kim Jong-un pledged to "de-escalate the Korean Peninsula," and President Trump mentioned a joint statement that pledged North Korea's security.
North Korea has been looking for a formal end to the 1950–53 Korean War, but the U.S. has said North Korea must first give up its nuclear weapons.
The U.S. has also vetoed calls to ease tough international sanctions on North Korea.
"The U.S. advocates 'denuclearization first', increases the level of pressure with sanctions in order to achieve its goals in a coercive way, and opposes the 'declaration of war.'"
“The recognition that sanctions can bring us to our knees is the dream of people who are ignorant about us.
“But the problem is that our mistrust is deepening with continued sanctions.”
Foreign Minister Lee made no mention of President Trump's second-chance plans for a second U.S.-inititudated summit between Mr. Kim, which was highlighted by the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, he emphasized three meetings between Kim and South Korean President Moon Jae-in over the past five months, adding, "If the party on this escalation issue had been South Korea, the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula wouldn't have been this stalemate."
Even so, Lee Young-ho’s tone of speech was quite different from what the U.N. General Assembly last year said it was unavoidable to target the U.S. mainland with a North Korean rocket after “Devil President” Trump characterized Kim as a “rocket man” on a suicide mission.
Mr. Trump, who threatened to "completely destroy" North Korea last year at the United Nations this year, praised Mr. Kim for being a courageous man to take steps to disarmament, but said he still needs to do a lot of work until North Korea denuclearizes and that sanctions must be maintained.
“It doesn’t matter if it’s two years, three years or five months,” Trump said, adding that there’s no such time frame.
China and Russia insist they should compensate Pyongyang for the measures taken by the U.N. Security Council.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appeared before the U.N. Security Council on Wednesday and said, “We must continue to vigorously enforcing Security Council sanctions until we realize a complete and finally verified escalation of denuclearization.”
The Security Council unanimally tightened sanctions on North Korea since 2006 to halt funding for Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Apart from the U.N. General Assembly, Pompeo said he will visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second summit.
Mr. Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip has not been smooth.
He said progress had been made in July, but within a few hours North Korea denounced him as a "thug-like demand."
In a meeting with President Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dislodge its missile sites and its nuclear complex if the United States took "responsive measures."
He said Mr. Kim delivered "appropriate measures," such as Mr. Trump's promised security measures in Singapore and moves to normalize relations with the United States.
Harvard students take classes to get enough rest
A new course at Harvard this year will allow all undergraduates to sleep better in order to combat a growing percussion culture through caffeine-adjudicated "all-night."
One scholar found that students at one of the world’s top universities were often ignorant of the very basic things about how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and Brigham Women's Hospital, came up with this course, which he believes is the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired to begin the course after giving a talk about the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
At the end of the class, a little girl came up to me and said to me, "Why don't you listen to these teachings when you're in your senior year?"
"She said that no one had ever spoken to her about the importance of sleep, which surprised me,' he said in an interview with The Telegraph.
For the first time this year, this course will explain to students how a good sleep habit can help them in their studies and athletic performance, and how important it is to improve their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, a professor of mental and mental health at the Harvard School of Medicine, says the university has decided to introduce this process when it comes to finding that students experience severe sleep deprivation during the week.
A one-hour course involves a series of interactive tasks.
There's an image of a dormitory in a section where students can click on a coffee cup, curtains, a trainer and a book to hear about the effects of caffeinance and light and how exercise is affected by sleep deprivation, and the importance of bedtime routines.
In another chapter, participants were told how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
A map with a campus map and an interactive icon makes participants think about their daily lives.
“We know it’s not going to immediately change the behavior of our students.
However, we believe they have a right to know, just as you have the right to know the health effects of smoking cigarettes," Professor Chaszler added.
He added that a culture of self-reliance for 'scratzing the night' still exists, and the ever-increasing pressure on modern technology and students means that sleep deprivation is an increasing problem.
Because sleep deprivation puts the brain in starvation mode and makes you constantly feel hungry, it has to be a "secret weapon" for students to combat stress, fatigue, and anxiety in order not to gain weight.
Nineteen-year-old Raymond So studied chemistry and physical biology in california helped Professor Cheysler design the course while taking one of his first classes at Harvard last year.
He said the course opened his eyes and inspired him to pursue courses all over the campus.
The next step is to require all graduate students to complete a similar learning program before entering a competitive institution.
Professor Kaiszler recommended that students should consider setting an alarm when they will go to bed and when they will wake up, heeding the detrimental effects of ‘blue light’ emitted by e-screens and LED light, which can cause sleep problems by getting the rhythm of your cycle out of a steady state.
