It’s normal for foreign governments to advertise to promote trade, but China and the U.S. are stuck in an escalating trade war that has now seen them impose tariffs on each other’s imports.
In the early days of the trade war, China’s retaliatory tariffs were intended to hit exporters such as Iowa, who had supported Trump’s Republicans and U.S. experts.
U.S. Ambassador Terry Branstad to the U.S. and the governor of Iowa, a major exporter of Chinese agricultural products, say Beijing has hurt U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
In an opinion letter published in the Des Moines Register on Sunday, Branstad wrote that China is "doubling down on harassment by putting out propaganda ads in our own free press."
"The Chinese government is harnessing its tradition of freedom of speech and freedom of speech that the U.S. cherishes by placing paid ads on the Deathmoin Register while disseminating propaganda," Branstad wrote.
"On the other hand," he wrote, "there will be limited voices of dissent here in the newspapers in Beijing, and given that the media is firmly in the hands of the Chinese Communist Party, it will not reflect the dissimilar opinions that the Chinese people have on an economic trajectory they may be concerned about."
He added that he had "abandoned the publication by one of China's leading newspapers," but he didn't say which.
