World Wide Web Plans founder Google to launch a new internet to acquire Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the founder of the World Wide Web, is starting a new company to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt, the latest project in this technological legend, Inrupt is a company based on Berners-Lee's open-source platform Solid.
Solid allows the user to choose where their data is stored and what kind of people have access to it.
Berners-Lee joked in an in-house interview with the Fast Company that Inrupt's intention was "world domination."
"We've got to do it now," he said of new businesses.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to allow people to create their own “personal online data stores” or PODs.
This can include a contact list, a task list, scheduling, a music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It's just like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in one browser at the same time.
What is unique about a personal online data store is that it is entirely up to you to what kind of information you can access.
Companies call this "personal empowerment through data."
According to the company’s CEO John Bruce, the idea for Inrupt is to provide resources, processes and appropriate technology to help the company use Solid products for everyone.
The company now consists of Berners-Lee, a security platform acquired by Bruce and IBM, some on-the-job developers contracted to work on the project, and a community of volunteer coders.
Starting this week, technology developers from all over the world will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on Inrupt websites.
Berners-by-S-Jerters Lee says he and his team aren’t talking to Facebook and Google about whether all of their business models will introduce a complete change that’s fully upgraded overnight.
“We’re not asking for their permission.”
“The mission is to provide an ecosystem conducive to protecting the integrity and quality of a new web built on top of commercial energy and Solid.”
At the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Berners-Lee changed the Internet by establishing the World Wide Web consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has had an influential voice in the net-neutrality controversies.
Even while starting Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Lab.
"I am unbelievably Optimistic about the next era on the Web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebrating the Victorian crucius cleric of World War I
During the First World War, the only British church cleric to win as a combatant in the Victorian crusade was celebrated in his homeland for more than a hundred years.
Sergeant Bernard Vann received the award in an attack on Bellengise and Lehaucourt on September 29, 1918.
But four days later, he was killed by a sniper and had no idea that he had been awarded the highest British medal.
His two grandsons unveiled the monument during a parade in Rushden (Northamptonshire, Northamptonshire) on Saturday.
Michael Vann, one of his grandchildren, said it was a "succeeding symbol" for the monument to reveal about his grandfather's award just a hundred years later.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918 lieutenant-to-be led his battalion "drun through a very thick fog and into a mesmerizing of artillery and machine guns" across the Canal de Saint-Quentin.
He later hurried up to the firing line, leading the line forward as the "Greatest Veterinarian," and then rushed into the field artillery alone to knock down three detachments.
He was killed by a German sniper on the 4th of October 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
Seventy-two-year-old Michael Van said his grandfather's actions were "pretentious, even though I know it's something I'll never be able to follow."
He and his older brother, Dr. James Vann, also laid a wreath after a parade led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
"It's a great honor to be involved in a parade procession," Michael Van said, adding that "the courage of a true hero is being demonstrated by the support he receives from so many people."
