Germany's new warship smokes again
The German Navy's newest frigate should have been commissioned in 2014 to replace an age-old Cold War-era warship, but because of its flawed system and the snowballing costs, it won't get there until at least next year, local media have reported.
According to Die Zeit's newspaper quoted a military spokesperson as saying, the approval of "Rheinland-Pfalz," a new frigate of the Baden-Euertemberg-class frigate, has been postponed for the first half of this year.
The ship should have joined the navy in 2014, but after a troubling delivery atrophied the fate of this ambitious project.
The four Baden-Uerttemberg-class ships ordered by the Navy will be replaced by the old Bremen-class frigate.
Some stealth technologies are believed to include powerful cannons, an array of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as radar reductions, infrared and sound signatures.
Other important features include a longer maintenance period, which means you should be able to place the latest frigates for up to two years on your home port.
But the ensuing procrastinating procrastinating escalation means the state-of-the-art wriggé, which would allow Germany to be able to project its power overseas, will already be obsolete by the time they get into service, Die Zeit said.
The unfortunate F125 frigate became a hot topic last year when the German navy officially rejected the ship's arrival and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It was the first time the Navy had completed its delivery and returned the ship to a shipyard.
While little is known about the background of the return, German media cited a number of conclusive “software and hardware glitches” that made warships obsolete when deployed on combat missions.
Software deficiencies were of particular importance because the Baden-Uerttemberg-class vessel would be operated by about one-and-a-thousand sailors with only half of its manpower on an old Bremen-class.
In addition, it has been found that because the ship is dramatically overweight, its performance is degraded and the Navy's ability to further upgrades is limited.
“Rheinland-Pfalz” is believed to be twice as heavy as a similar-grade ship used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to hardware glitches, the price tag of the entire project, including the training of the crew, is also troubling.
That’s more than $36bn.
In view of the recent warnings that Germany’s naval forces are dwindling, the problems that captivate the most recent frigates are particularly important.
Earlier in the year, the German parliament's defense chief, Hans-Peter Barters, acknowledged that the navy is actually "depleting its available ships."
The official said the old vessel had been dismantled, but no alternative vessels had been provided, so the issue was blowing up like a snowball over time.
He lamented that there was not one of the Baden-Euertemberg-class frigates that could join the navy.
The National Trust has intercepted the secret creatures of the bat.
A new piece of research is being carried out on a private property in the Scottish Highlands with the aim of uncovering how bats take advantage of this landscape when hunting for their prey.
We hope that this discovery will refresh the behavior of unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
A study by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow brown long-eared bats and Daubento bats in Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross, as well as common soprano Phipiistrells and soprano pipiistrels.
Special recording devices will be placed in key locations around the property to keep track of bat activity throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using hand-held detectors.
An expert sound analysis of all recordings will determine the frequency of bats and what species are doing.
Habitat maps and reports will be generated to create a detailed landscape painting of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, a nature conservation adviser at the NTS, is hoping to find out which habitats are most important for bats and how they are used by their respective species.
This information will help to determine the benefits of habitat management work such as pasture and how best to maintain the woodlands of bats and other related species.
The population of bats across Scotland and the United Kingdom has declined considerably over the past century.
They are under the threat of construction and development work that affect the loss of nests and habitat.
Wind turbines and lighting can also be dangerous, as this can cause not only the chemical treatment of flying paper and building materials, but also an attack on your pet.
A bat isn't really blind.
However, due to its nocturnal hunting habits, when it comes to catching prey, their ears are more useful than their eyes.
