Brexit: UK 'worst to leave and remortgage' for automakers' withdrawal
British Industry Minister Gregg Clark said the UK’s industry position, which has held a leading position in the automotive manufacturing sector so far after Brexit, will plummet and “regret.”
A Toyota official also told the BBC it would temporarily suspend production at a plant in Burnaston, near Derby, if the U.K. leaves the EU without a deal, Mr. Clarke raised "a voice of concern."
"We need to come up with an agreement," Mr Clarke stressed.
According to a Japanese automaker, a post-Brexit process without an agreement could lead to delays in cross-strategy and reduce jobs.
The Burnastone plant, which manufactures Toyota Oris and Avensys, exported 90 per cent of the 150,000 cars produced last year to member states of the European Union.
"In my view, if the U.K. gets Brexit done at the end of March, we have no choice but to stop production," Marvin Kook, head of management at Burnastone.
Other British automakers, such as Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land rover, are also unsettled about leaving the EU without an agreement on cross-border trade.
For example, a BMW spokeswoman said it was scheduled to close a mini-factory in Oxford starting next month after Brexit.
Looking at the automaker’s statement, there is great concern that there will be a huge setback in the supply chain if Brexit goes through without an agreement.
Toyota’s production line does not have a “JIT” method of stacking parts and building a vehicle, but a method of ordering it at that time, so the parts needed to produce an ordered car arrive every thirty-seven minutes from suppliers in the United Kingdom and the EU.
If the U.K. leaves for a no-deal Brexit on March 29th, there will be problems with parts crossing the border, which will lead to delays and insufficiencies in the supply of parts.
Production would be halted because it would not be possible to store more than a day's worth of inventory at a Toyota plant in Dervishire, a factory official said.
Clark noted that when Prime Minister Theresa May plans a future relationship with the EU, "we need to concretely design so that we don't go through an unnecessary verification process on the border."
"There is a desperate need for a consultation. In order to seize not only the success you see right in front of your eyes, as well as the chances of a post-Brex/Brexit, we need to come up with the best possible consultations," Mr Clarke said in today's programme on BBC Radio Four.
“Considering the concerns of Toyota as well as other automakers, we need to be able to ensure that the supply chain structure that has been successfully operational up to date is in place in the future.”
Toyota says it’s hard to predict for how long it’s going to stop production, and in the long-suffocating years, it’s going to be less competitive in its production because of additional costs.
Peter Sobalis, vice-president of unions who have worked at the Burnaston plant for twenty-four years, said union members were very worried, adding, “In my experience, a once-lost job has never been reinstated.”
A government spokesperson said: "We will implement an accurate and reliable plan to ensure a well-established relationship with the EU in the future."
