A heartfelt woman attaches a card to prevent her from committing suicide in her legs.
The two women who lost a loved one by suicide are working to prevent others from committing suicide.
Charon Davies and Kelly Humphries have been glued over a Welsh bridge with an inspirational message and a card with a phone number so that people can ask for support.
Davis's son, Tyler, began suffering from depression at the age of thirteen and committed suicide at the age of eighteen.
“I don’t want any parents to feel the emotions I feel every day,” she said.
Forty-five-year-old Davis, who lives in Reidney, said her son was a promising chef with an infectious smile.
“They all knew about his son’s smile.
“They said his smile would light up a room.”
“He was in a really dark place, so he gave up his job before he died.”
For the first time, Tyler's brother, who was eleven years old at the time, found the older brother who had taken his own life for the first time.
"I'm worried there's going to have a domino effect over and over again," Mr. Davis said.
Mr. Davis made a card, and he said, "To let people know that there are people that you can go and meet and talk to, even your friends."
“Don’t be quiet, you need to have a conversation.”
Mr Humphreys, who had been friends with Mr. Davis for several years, lost his fifteen-year-old co-partner, Mark, shortly after his mother's death.
"He didn't say that he was in a bad mood or depressed," she said.
“Two days before Christmas we noticed a change in his attitude.
“He didn’t make any eye contact with the children when they opened their presents on Christmas Day.”
She said his death was a big trauma for them, but they had to get over it. It punches a hole in the hearts of their families.
It rips us apart.
“We all need to move forward and fight.”
