Number of defectors 'reduced' under Kim Jong-un's regime
The number of North Korean defectors coming into South Korea seven years ago has declined since Kim Jong-un took power, a lawmaker in South Korea said.
Citing data from South Korea's Unification Ministry, Senator Park Byeong-seok said there were 1,127 defectors last year, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Park said tightening border controls between North Korea and China and the increase in the amount demanded by businessmen helping to defect were the main factors.
North Korea has made no public comment on it.
The vast majority of North Korean defectors are eventually granted South Korean citizenship.
The South Korean government says that more than thirty thousand North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most defect through China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the demilitarized zone (DMZ), which has heavy-frends between North and South Korea.
China considers North Korean defectors to be illegals rather than as refugees and forcibly repatriates them.
Technically speaking, relations between North and South Korea, which are still at war, have improved markedly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang for talks focused on deadlocked escalating escalating escalating escalation.
This comes after a historic meeting between U.S. U.S. U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim in Singapore in June.
But on Saturday, North Korea's foreign minister, Ri Yong-ho, denounced U.S. sanctions, saying there had been no progress since.
In a speech to the U.N. General Assembly in New York, he said, “If there’s no trust in the U.S., there’s no confidence in our national security, and there’s no way we can unilaterally disarmament first.”
