Concentrations decrease in red tide pinra, Manate and Sarasota
A recent report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows that red tide concentrations are generally declining in parts of Tampa Bay.
According to the commission, red tides have been observed everywhere in the areas of Pinera, Manate, Sarasota, Charlotte, and Collier. This means that concentrations are deteriorating.
The red tide stretches about 130miles of coastline from northern Pineras to Southern Lee County.
Hirglomese pieces are found about ten miles from Hillsborough County, but in less area than last week.
Red tide was also observed in Pasco County.
Last week, median or high concentrations were reported in Pinesborough counties, low or high concentrations in Hillsborough County, normal or high-concentration in Manate County, normal or high-concentration in Sarasota counties and off-shore, normal or medium-concentration in Charlotte County. Low-concentration or low concentrations in Collier County were reported.
Respiratory diseases continue to be reported in Pinra, Manate, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
No respiratory illnesses were reported last week in northwestern Florida.
